{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "מי לך", :translit "mi lakh", :gloss "who to you"} {:he "מי לך פה כי חצבת לך פה קבר", :translit "mi lakh poh ki chatzavta lakh poh kever", :gloss "who to you here, for you hewed for yourself here a grave"} {:he "מי לך פה חתן ובניך", :translit "mi lakh poh chatan u-vanekha", :gloss "who to you here, a groom and your sons"} {:he "מי לך כל המחנה", :translit "mi lakh kol ha-machaneh", :gloss "who to you, all the camp"} {:he "מי לך פה בנים ובנות", :translit "mi lakh poh banim u-vanot", :gloss "who to you here, sons and daughters"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מי לך" "מי לך פה כי חצבת לך פה קבר" "מי לך פה חתן ובניך"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase מי לך appears three times in the Bible, and the Midrash links all three to Esau's encounter with Jacob. When Jacob sent a massive camp to meet Esau (Genesis 33:8), Esau asked מי לך כל המחנה. The Midrash explains that bands of ministering angels struck Esau's men until graves were hewn for the dead — connecting to the verse מי לך פה כי חצבת לך פה קבר (Numbers 20:30). The third occurrence, מי לך פה חתן ובניך (Judges 15:6), further extends the pattern. A separate midrashic tradition holds that Jacob and Esau contested over two worlds while still in Rebecca's womb, with Jacob choosing the World to Come, which has no procreation. When Esau saw Jacob's children he seized on this: if Jacob had chosen the World to Come, how did he have sons?", :sources ["Genesis 33:8" "Numbers 20:30" "Judges 15:6" "Bereshit Rabbah 78:9" "Megillah 6a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מי לך" "מי לך פה כי חצבת לך פה קבר" "מי לך פה חתן ובניך"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "מי לך", :refs ["Genesis 19:12" "Genesis 33:8"], :n 2, :lens :exact} {:phrase "מי לך פה כי חצבת לך פה קבר", :refs ["Isaiah 22:16"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "מי לך פה חתן ובניך", :refs ["Genesis 19:12"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :